<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>WinExe</OutputType>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
    <AssemblyName>PasteJump</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>PasteJump.App</RootNamespace>
    <ApplicationManifest>app.manifest</ApplicationManifest>
    <AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
    <ApplicationIcon>Assets\pastejump.ico</ApplicationIcon>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Self-contained single-file publish: one PasteJump.exe, no .NET runtime required on the target machine
    and nothing beside it.

    Trimming and NativeAOT are BOTH unsupported with WPF - do not enable them. That is what fixes the floor
    at tens of megabytes: unlike a .NET Framework app, which can be a few MB because Windows already carries
    its runtime, this has to bring the whole of .NET and WPF with it.

    EnableCompressionInSingleFile roughly halves the result, 143 MB down to 65 MB, and is faster as well as
    smaller - it is not the trade it looks like. Instrumented start-up, process launch to tray icon: 2,873 ms
    first run and ~1,150 ms warm compressed, against 3,416 ms and ~1,690 ms uncompressed. Reading 143 MB costs
    more than decompressing 65 MB.

    IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract is required, because WPF's native libraries cannot be loaded from
    inside the bundle. Note what it does NOT need to be joined by:

    IncludeAllContentForSelfExtract is deliberately ABSENT, and was here once on the incorrect belief that
    without it the Assets\*.ico files would sit loose beside the exe. They do not - content is bundled either
    way, and extracted either way. What the flag adds is extraction of every *managed assembly* too, which
    .NET can load straight from the bundle and has no reason to write to disk. Measured on this app: 9.76 MB
    extracted to %TEMP% without it against 133.95 MB with it, 8 files against hundreds, and the icons present
    in both. It cost roughly ten seconds on a first run and bought nothing.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <RuntimeIdentifier>win-x64</RuntimeIdentifier>
    <SelfContained>true</SelfContained>
    <PublishReadyToRun>true</PublishReadyToRun>
    <PublishTrimmed>false</PublishTrimmed>
    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>

    <PublishSingleFile>true</PublishSingleFile>
    <IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>true</IncludeNativeLibrariesForSelfExtract>
    <EnableCompressionInSingleFile>true</EnableCompressionInSingleFile>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    The WPF implicit-usings set does not include System.IO (unlike the plain library set), and
    this app touches the filesystem in several places. Added once here rather than as a using
    directive repeated across every view and service.
  -->
  <!--
    The UI smoke harness reaches the tray menu's item list and its builder, both of which are internal and should
    stay that way: the alternative is making four more types public purely so a test can see them, which is how
    an application's surface stops describing what other code may use.

    Only the harness. Nothing else in the solution needs it, and the App assembly is not signed, so no public key
    is involved.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="PasteJump.UiSmoke" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Using Include="System.IO" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Build timestamp, shown in the About window under the version.

    It cannot come from the PE header: Deterministic is on in Directory.Build.props, so the field that used
    to hold a link time holds a content hash instead. Nor from the exe's own file time, which is what a copy
    or an unzip decides rather than what the build did.

    Two consequences worth knowing before moving this:

    - Only this project carries it, not Directory.Build.props. The value changes on every evaluation, so the
      generated attribute file changes, so the project recompiles on every build. Confining that to the entry
      assembly keeps Core, Interop, Import and the test projects incremental.
    - It does make THIS assembly non-reproducible, which is a deliberate exception to Deterministic rather
      than an oversight: two builds of the same commit differ in this one string. AppVersion falls back to
      the executable's file time when the attribute is missing, so a host without it still shows something.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <AssemblyAttribute Include="System.Reflection.AssemblyMetadataAttribute">
      <_Parameter1>BuildTimestampUtc</_Parameter1>
      <_Parameter2>$([System.DateTime]::UtcNow.ToString("O"))</_Parameter2>
    </AssemblyAttribute>
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    pastejump.ico is shipped two ways on purpose, and each one is reachable only by its own mechanism -
    which is why this looks redundant and is not.

    ApplicationIcon    puts it in the PE header, all nine frames. That is what Explorer, the taskbar,
                       Alt+Tab and Task Manager read - and, because no window sets Window.Icon, what
                       every window falls back to as well. Windows picks the frame per surface and per
                       DPI, which is the one approach that cannot pick wrong. NOT reachable through a
                       pack:// URI, so no code or XAML can read this copy.
    Resource           embeds it in the assembly, where TrayIconArt reads the bytes by pack:// URI and
                       hands one frame to CreateIconFromResourceEx at the shell's current small-icon
                       metric. Extracting the PE copy instead would give a fixed 16x16 that the shell
                       upscales, and at 150% scaling - where it wants 24 - that is visibly blurry.

    These were Content items until 2026-08-12, which put three loose .ico files beside the exe. The
    reason was that only LoadImage(LR_LOADFROMFILE) honoured a requested size, and it needs a path;
    CreateIconFromResourceEx takes a size and raw bytes, so the files no longer have to exist on disk.
    What that fixes is not the 54 KB: a portable copy unzipped without its Assets folder lost its tray
    icon, and with no main window that left no way to reach the application at all.

    There was a third mechanism, a Resource item for the pack:// URI behind Shared.xaml's AppIcon key.
    It is gone with that key: binding Window.Icon to a BitmapImage forces one frame to serve every size,
    and every choice of frame was wrong somewhere. See the note at the top of Themes/Shared.xaml.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <Resource Include="Assets\pastejump.ico" />

    <!--
      Large single-frame mark for anything that displays the logo at a chosen size, as opposed to handing it
      to Windows as a window icon. Resource only: it is reached by pack:// URI, never as a file.
    -->
    <Resource Include="Assets\pastejump-256.png" />

    <!--
      Greyed and amber variants for the tray while PasteJump is disabled or paused. Unlike the pair of
      monochrome glyphs this project used to carry, these are not a light/dark theme split - they are three
      application states, and picking between them is not something a single icon can do. Neither is in the PE
      header, which holds one icon group only, so embedding is what makes them reachable without loose files.
    -->
    <Resource Include="Assets\pastejump-disabled.ico" />
    <Resource Include="Assets\pastejump-paused.ico" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PasteJump.Core\PasteJump.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PasteJump.Interop\PasteJump.Interop.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\PasteJump.Import\PasteJump.Import.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
